{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_e6rucq6s473rh7jzy6n2dyfhgamst3iq7ja63jpprgy5ptdokz2q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01H72oPjKZCR9K1aaNmRDtux","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-UFFe5s/project/c_src/README.md"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1568,"cache_read_input_tokens":21139,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1568,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14ac4984-cc43-4df6-a097-f68231e01522","uuid":"c98752ee-ccec-47a2-836c-6c012e20e6b0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01H72oPjKZCR9K1aaNmRDtux","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCH-Trie\n2\t=======\n3\t\n4\t*CH-Trie* is the official C library of the *coordinate hash trie*.\n5\tSee [the *Coordinate Hash Trie* section](#coordinate-hash-trie).\n6\t\n7\tCH-Trie is designed for arbitrary symbols, not just characters.\n8\tInstead of providing high-level string set/dictionary interfaces,\n9\tCH-Trie provides low-level interfaces making you implement your own trie-based data structures.\n10\tSee [the *Usage* section](#usage).\n11\t\n12\t\n13\t**Table of Contents**\n14\t\n15\t* [Coordinate Hash Trie](#coordinate-hash-trie)\n16\t* [Installation](#installation)\n17\t* [Usage](#usage)\n18\t* [Example: Implementing a String Set](#example-implementing-a-string-set)\n19\t* [Following News](#following-news)\n20\t* [Related Projects](#related-projects)\n21\t\n22\tCoordinate Hash Trie\n23\t--------------------\n24\t\n25\tThe coordinate hash trie is a trie variant balancing between time, space, and simplicity.\n26\t\n27\tThe basic idea is very simple.\n28\tWe use a global hash table to store all edges in a Trie.\n29\tEach edge is stored as a dictionary item `(from, symb)->to`.\n30\t\n31\tWe use a special hash function:\n32\t\n33\t\th(from, symb) = (from*m + symb) mod H\n34\t\n35\t, where `m` is the size of the alphabet, and `H` is the number of slots in the hash table. For a trie with `n` nodes, we take `H = (n-1)/alpha` where `alpha` is the constant load factor. **No rehashing, resizing, or reallocation is required.**\n36\t\n37\tWe could prove that **the time complexity of transition from one node to another, is O(1) for the average case and O(m) for the worst case**. **The space complexity is O(n), unrelated to** `m`.\n38\t\n39\tSee [my paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03690) for the analysis and proof.\n40\t\n41\tCompared to other compressed trie variants like *double array trie*, or using `n` hash tables each for a node, this variant provides **stable space consumption** and is very **easy to implement**.\n42\t\n43\tInstallation\n44\t------------\n45\t\n46\tCH-Trie requires a C89 compiler and POSIX `errno` definitions.\n47\t\n48\tTo install CH-Trie as header files and a static library into the system:\n49\t\n50\t\tmake\n51\t\tsudo make install\n52\t\n53\tBy default, CH-Trie is installed to `/usr/local`.\n54\tTo link CH-Trie, use the `-l chtrie` option on your compiler.\n55\t\n56\tIt's also simple to embed CH-Trie into your project.\n57\tJust include `chtrie.h`.\n58\tCompile and link `chtrie.c`.\n59\t\n60\tUsage\n61\t-----\n62\t\n63\tInclude the following header files to use CH-Trie:\n64\t\n65\t\t#include <stddef.h>\n66\t\t#include \"chtrie.h\"\n67\t\n68\t- `typedef ... chtrie`\n69\t\n70\t\tA `chtrie` instance represents a trie.\n71\t\n72\t- `chtrie *chtrie_alloc(size_t n, size_t m)`\n73\t\n74\t\tAllocate a trie with at most `n` nodes, and the alphabet size `m`.\n75\t\n76\t\tIf `n` or `m` is less than 1, they will be regulated to 1.\n77\t\n78\t\tNodes in the trie are indexed by non-negative integers less than `n`.\n79\t\tThe root node is indexed by 0.\n80\t\tSymbols are non-negative integers less than `m`.\n81\t\n82\t\tUpon success, return a pointer to the trie.\n83\t\tOtherwise, return `NULL` and set `errno`.\n84\t\n85\t- `int chtrie_walk(chtrie *tr, int from, int sym, int creat)`\n86\t\n87\t\tWalk from one node to its child.\n88\t\n89\t\tIf the child didn't exist and `creat` is non-zero,\n90\t\ta new node will be created.\n91\t\t\n92\t\tUpon the child is found or created, return the index of the child.\n93\t\tOtherwise, return -1.\n94\t\tIf `creat` is non-zero and this routine fails, `errno` will be set.\n95\t\t\n96\t- `void chtrie_del(chtrie *tr, int from, int sym)`\n97\t\n98\t\tDelete a child node.\n99\t\n100\t\tThe child node must be a leaf if it exists,\n101\t\tor the behavior is undefined.\n102\t\t\n103\t\tIf the child doesn't exist, the trie shall be left unchanged.\n104\t\n105\t- `void chtrie_free(chtrie *tr)`\n106\t\n107\t\tFree the trie.\n108\t\n109\tExample: Implementing a String Set\n110\t-------------------------------\n111\t\n112\tThe following program builds a trie with words from the `dict` array excluding those that are also in the `stop` array.\n113\t\n114\tAfter the trie is built, it reads lines from the standard input.\n115\tFor each line, the program prints `1` if the line is in the trie.\n116\tOtherwise, the program prints `0`.\n117\t\n118\tThis example illustrates the common usage of CH-Trie, e.g. how to associate additional information with a node.\n119\t\n120\t\t#include <stddef.h>\n121\t\t#include <string.h>\n122\t\t#include <stdlib.h>\n123\t\t#include <stdio.h>\n124\t\t#include \"chtrie.h\"\n125\t\t#define fatal(s) do { perror(s); exit(-1); } while (0)\n126\t\t#define N 65536\n127\t\t#define M 256\n128\t\n129\t\tstatic char *dict[] = {\n130\t\t\t\"\", \"he\", \"the\", \"she\", \"his\", \"hers\", \"a\", \"an\", \"this\", \"that\"\n131\t\t};\n132\t\tstatic char *stop[] = { \"the\", \"an\", \"a\" };\n133\t\n134\t\tstatic chtrie *tr;\n135\t\n136\t\tstatic int term[N];\t/* is `i` a  termination node */\n137\t\tstatic int nchild[N];\t/* number of children of `i` */\n138\t\n139\t\tstatic void add(char *s);\n140\t\tstatic void del(char *s);\n141\t\tstatic int query(char *s);\n142\t\n143\t\tint main(void)\n144\t\t{\n145\t\t\tstatic char line[N];\n146\t\t\tint i, j;\n147\t\n148\t\t\tif (!(tr = chtrie_alloc(N, M)))\n149\t\t\t\tfatal(\"chtrie_alloc\");\n150\t\t\tfor (i = 0; i < sizeof dict / sizeof dict[0]; i++)\n151\t\t\t\tadd(dict[i]);\n152\t\t\tfor (i = 0; i < sizeof stop / sizeof stop[0]; i++)\n153\t\t\t\tdel(stop[i]);\n154\t\n155\t\t\twhile (fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin)) {\n156\t\t\t\tline[strcspn(line, \"\\n\")] = '\\0';\n157\t\t\t\tprintf(\"%d\\n\", query(line) ? 1 : 0);\n158\t\t\t}\n159\t\t\tchtrie_free(tr);\n160\t\t\treturn 0;\n161\t\t}\n162\t\n163\t\tstatic void add(char *s)\n164\t\t{\n165\t\t\tint it;\n166\t\n167\t\t\tit = 0;\n168\t\t\twhile (*s) {\n169\t\t\t\tif (chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s, 0) == -1)\n170\t\t\t\t\tnchild[it]++;\n171\t\t\t\tif ((it = chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s, 1)) == -1)\n172\t\t\t\t\tfatal(\"chtrie_walk\");\n173\t\t\t\ts++;\n174\t\t\t}\n175\t\t\tterm[it] = 1;\n176\t\t}\n177\t\n178\t\tstatic void del(char *s)\n179\t\t{\n180\t\t\tstatic int nodes[N], symbs[N], n;\t/* trace the path */\n181\t\t\tint it;\n182\t\n183\t\t\tn = 0;\n184\t\t\tit = 0;\n185\t\t\twhile (it >= 0 && *s) {\n186\t\t\t\tnodes[n] = it;\n187\t\t\t\tsymbs[n] = *s;\n188\t\t\t\tn++;\n189\t\t\t\tit = chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s++, 0);\n190\t\t\t}\n191\t\t\tif (it < 0 || !term[it])\n192\t\t\t\treturn;\n193\t\t\tterm[it] = 0;\n194\t\t\twhile (it > 0 && !term[it] && nchild[it] == 0) {\n195\t\t\t\tn--;\n196\t\t\t\tchtrie_del(tr, nodes[n], symbs[n]);\n197\t\t\t\tit = nodes[n];\n198\t\t\t\tnchild[it]--;\n199\t\t\t}\n200\t\t}\n201\t\n202\t\tstatic int query(char *s)\n203\t\t{\n204\t\t\tint it;\n205\t\n206\t\t\tit = 0;\n207\t\t\twhile (it >= 0 && *s)\n208\t\t\t\tit = chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s++, 0);\n209\t\t\treturn it >= 0 && term[it];\n210\t\t}\n211\t\n212\tFollowing News\n213\t--------------\n214\t\n215\t- <https://www.dyx.name>\n216\t- [Twitter](https://twitter.com/dongyx2)\n217\t\n218\tRelated Projects\n219\t----------------\n220\t\n221\t- [LibACA](https://github.com/dongyx/libaca): A C Library of Aho-Corasick Algorithm Based on the Coordinate Hash Trie\n222\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14ac4984-cc43-4df6-a097-f68231e01522","uuid":"bb787697-6951-41c5-a7c6-2cf21dc266fc","timestamp":"2026-06-06T02:50:18.242Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-UFFe5s/project/c_src/README.md","content":"CH-Trie\n=======\n\n*CH-Trie* is the official C library of the *coordinate hash trie*.\nSee [the *Coordinate Hash Trie* section](#coordinate-hash-trie).\n\nCH-Trie is designed for arbitrary symbols, not just characters.\nInstead of providing high-level string set/dictionary interfaces,\nCH-Trie provides low-level interfaces making you implement your own trie-based data structures.\nSee [the *Usage* section](#usage).\n\n\n**Table of Contents**\n\n* [Coordinate Hash Trie](#coordinate-hash-trie)\n* [Installation](#installation)\n* [Usage](#usage)\n* [Example: Implementing a String Set](#example-implementing-a-string-set)\n* [Following News](#following-news)\n* [Related Projects](#related-projects)\n\nCoordinate Hash Trie\n--------------------\n\nThe coordinate hash trie is a trie variant balancing between time, space, and simplicity.\n\nThe basic idea is very simple.\nWe use a global hash table to store all edges in a Trie.\nEach edge is stored as a dictionary item `(from, symb)->to`.\n\nWe use a special hash function:\n\n\th(from, symb) = (from*m + symb) mod H\n\n, where `m` is the size of the alphabet, and `H` is the number of slots in the hash table. For a trie with `n` nodes, we take `H = (n-1)/alpha` where `alpha` is the constant load factor. **No rehashing, resizing, or reallocation is required.**\n\nWe could prove that **the time complexity of transition from one node to another, is O(1) for the average case and O(m) for the worst case**. **The space complexity is O(n), unrelated to** `m`.\n\nSee [my paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03690) for the analysis and proof.\n\nCompared to other compressed trie variants like *double array trie*, or using `n` hash tables each for a node, this variant provides **stable space consumption** and is very **easy to implement**.\n\nInstallation\n------------\n\nCH-Trie requires a C89 compiler and POSIX `errno` definitions.\n\nTo install CH-Trie as header files and a static library into the system:\n\n\tmake\n\tsudo make install\n\nBy default, CH-Trie is installed to `/usr/local`.\nTo link CH-Trie, use the `-l chtrie` option on your compiler.\n\nIt's also simple to embed CH-Trie into your project.\nJust include `chtrie.h`.\nCompile and link `chtrie.c`.\n\nUsage\n-----\n\nInclude the following header files to use CH-Trie:\n\n\t#include <stddef.h>\n\t#include \"chtrie.h\"\n\n- `typedef ... chtrie`\n\n\tA `chtrie` instance represents a trie.\n\n- `chtrie *chtrie_alloc(size_t n, size_t m)`\n\n\tAllocate a trie with at most `n` nodes, and the alphabet size `m`.\n\n\tIf `n` or `m` is less than 1, they will be regulated to 1.\n\n\tNodes in the trie are indexed by non-negative integers less than `n`.\n\tThe root node is indexed by 0.\n\tSymbols are non-negative integers less than `m`.\n\n\tUpon success, return a pointer to the trie.\n\tOtherwise, return `NULL` and set `errno`.\n\n- `int chtrie_walk(chtrie *tr, int from, int sym, int creat)`\n\n\tWalk from one node to its child.\n\n\tIf the child didn't exist and `creat` is non-zero,\n\ta new node will be created.\n\t\n\tUpon the child is found or created, return the index of the child.\n\tOtherwise, return -1.\n\tIf `creat` is non-zero and this routine fails, `errno` will be set.\n\t\n- `void chtrie_del(chtrie *tr, int from, int sym)`\n\n\tDelete a child node.\n\n\tThe child node must be a leaf if it exists,\n\tor the behavior is undefined.\n\t\n\tIf the child doesn't exist, the trie shall be left unchanged.\n\n- `void chtrie_free(chtrie *tr)`\n\n\tFree the trie.\n\nExample: Implementing a String Set\n-------------------------------\n\nThe following program builds a trie with words from the `dict` array excluding those that are also in the `stop` array.\n\nAfter the trie is built, it reads lines from the standard input.\nFor each line, the program prints `1` if the line is in the trie.\nOtherwise, the program prints `0`.\n\nThis example illustrates the common usage of CH-Trie, e.g. how to associate additional information with a node.\n\n\t#include <stddef.h>\n\t#include <string.h>\n\t#include <stdlib.h>\n\t#include <stdio.h>\n\t#include \"chtrie.h\"\n\t#define fatal(s) do { perror(s); exit(-1); } while (0)\n\t#define N 65536\n\t#define M 256\n\n\tstatic char *dict[] = {\n\t\t\"\", \"he\", \"the\", \"she\", \"his\", \"hers\", \"a\", \"an\", \"this\", \"that\"\n\t};\n\tstatic char *stop[] = { \"the\", \"an\", \"a\" };\n\n\tstatic chtrie *tr;\n\n\tstatic int term[N];\t/* is `i` a  termination node */\n\tstatic int nchild[N];\t/* number of children of `i` */\n\n\tstatic void add(char *s);\n\tstatic void del(char *s);\n\tstatic int query(char *s);\n\n\tint main(void)\n\t{\n\t\tstatic char line[N];\n\t\tint i, j;\n\n\t\tif (!(tr = chtrie_alloc(N, M)))\n\t\t\tfatal(\"chtrie_alloc\");\n\t\tfor (i = 0; i < sizeof dict / sizeof dict[0]; i++)\n\t\t\tadd(dict[i]);\n\t\tfor (i = 0; i < sizeof stop / sizeof stop[0]; i++)\n\t\t\tdel(stop[i]);\n\n\t\twhile (fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin)) {\n\t\t\tline[strcspn(line, \"\\n\")] = '\\0';\n\t\t\tprintf(\"%d\\n\", query(line) ? 1 : 0);\n\t\t}\n\t\tchtrie_free(tr);\n\t\treturn 0;\n\t}\n\n\tstatic void add(char *s)\n\t{\n\t\tint it;\n\n\t\tit = 0;\n\t\twhile (*s) {\n\t\t\tif (chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s, 0) == -1)\n\t\t\t\tnchild[it]++;\n\t\t\tif ((it = chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s, 1)) == -1)\n\t\t\t\tfatal(\"chtrie_walk\");\n\t\t\ts++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tterm[it] = 1;\n\t}\n\n\tstatic void del(char *s)\n\t{\n\t\tstatic int nodes[N], symbs[N], n;\t/* trace the path */\n\t\tint it;\n\n\t\tn = 0;\n\t\tit = 0;\n\t\twhile (it >= 0 && *s) {\n\t\t\tnodes[n] = it;\n\t\t\tsymbs[n] = *s;\n\t\t\tn++;\n\t\t\tit = chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s++, 0);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (it < 0 || !term[it])\n\t\t\treturn;\n\t\tterm[it] = 0;\n\t\twhile (it > 0 && !term[it] && nchild[it] == 0) {\n\t\t\tn--;\n\t\t\tchtrie_del(tr, nodes[n], symbs[n]);\n\t\t\tit = nodes[n];\n\t\t\tnchild[it]--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tstatic int query(char *s)\n\t{\n\t\tint it;\n\n\t\tit = 0;\n\t\twhile (it >= 0 && *s)\n\t\t\tit = chtrie_walk(tr, it, (unsigned char)*s++, 0);\n\t\treturn it >= 0 && term[it];\n\t}\n\nFollowing News\n--------------\n\n- <https://www.dyx.name>\n- [Twitter](https://twitter.com/dongyx2)\n\nRelated Projects\n----------------\n\n- [LibACA](https://github.com/dongyx/libaca): A C Library of Aho-Corasick Algorithm Based on the Coordinate Hash Trie\n","numLines":222,"startLine":1,"totalLines":222}}}
